  "answer_text": "The prominent bump on the front of the throat known as the **Adam\u2019s apple** is actually the **laryngeal prominence of the thyroid cartilage**. This cartilage wraps around the larynx (voice box) to protect the vocal cords. \n\nMen generally have much larger and more visible Adam\u2019s apples than women due to a combination of **hormones, anatomical angles, and puberty**. \n\n---\n\n### 1. The Role of Testosterone during Puberty\nBefore puberty, boys and girls have similarly sized larynxes and thyroid cartilages. However, when puberty begins:\n* **In males:** A surge in the hormone **testosterone** triggers rapid growth of the larynx and the vocal cords. The vocal cords grow longer and thicker (which is why a male\u2019s voice deepens). To accommodate and protect these larger vocal cords, the surrounding thyroid cartilage must also grow significantly larger.\n* **In females:** While the larynx grows slightly during puberty, the surge in estrogen does not stimulate the same degree of cartilage or vocal cord expansion. Consequently, a female\u2019s voice remains higher, and her thyroid cartilage stays relatively small.\n\n### 2. The Angle of the Cartilage\nThe primary reason the Adam's apple physically protrudes in men but not in women comes down to geometry. The thyroid cartilage is made of two plates that meet at the front of the throat to form a \"V\" shape. \n* **In males:** The plates join at a sharp, narrow angle of **roughly 90 degrees**. This sharp angle causes the cartilage to tilt forward and protrude outward against the skin, creating the visible \"apple\" shape.\n* **In females:** The plates join at a much wider, more obtuse angle of **roughly 120 degrees**. Because the angle is so wide, the cartilage sits flatter against the neck and is generally invisible from the outside.\n\n### 3. Do women have an Adam's apple?\n**Yes, everyone has thyroid cartilage**, meaning everyone anatomically has the structure that makes up the Adam's apple. However, it is usually only visible in women under specific circumstances:\n* **Low body fat:** Very thin women may have a visible laryngeal prominence simply because there is less subcutaneous fat covering the neck.\n* **Hormonal differences:** Some women naturally produce higher levels of testosterone, which can cause their larynx to grow larger during puberty.\n* **Anatomical variation:** Like any physical feature, the size and shape of the throat cartilage naturally vary from person to person. \n\n### Cultural and Medical Significance\nBecause a prominent Adam's apple is a secondary sex characteristic associated with male puberty, it is often viewed as a masculine feature. \n\n* **Transgender men** who undergo testosterone hormone therapy will often experience growth of the larynx, which can cause their voice to deepen and a more prominent Adam's apple to develop. \n* **Transgender women** or individuals who prefer a smoother neck profile can undergo a cosmetic procedure called a **tracheal shave** (chondrolaryngoplasty). During this procedure, a surgeon shaves down the thyroid cartilage to reduce the size and prominence of the bump.",
